--TEST--
a `secret`-qualified value written at a `Core\Debug::dump` or `Core\Debug::render` call site is refused where it is written, on both bases and composed with `tainted` — `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`'s debug-dump sink, `rule:errors/record-transformations`'s redaction row
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:errors/record-transformations` states redaction as two halves of one rule about one record: a
// property whose *declared* type carries `secret` becomes a Redacted node, and
// a `secret` value handed straight to the dump is refused by `nvs check`. This
// file is the second half. It is a call-site rule because both members declare
// `mixed`, which a `secret string` satisfies — so this is the last point the
// qualifier is visible at all.
string $plain = "plain";
tainted string $t = "t";
secret string $s = "s";
secret bytes $sb = "s" as bytes;
secret tainted string $st = "s";

// The accepted half first, so a position that stops being accepted fails here
// rather than as a missing refusal. `rule:errors/record-transformations`'s closing paragraph is why
// `tainted` is not on this list: the record frames a dumped value as data and
// the rendering is what makes it safe, so taint flows in freely.
Core\Debug::dump($plain);
Core\Debug::dump($t, $plain);
echo Core\Debug::render($t), "\n";

// `rule:security/secret-qualifier`'s `secret` on its own, at the member the ADR names.
Core\Debug::dump($s);

// The refusal names the argument it is about, not the call: a `dump` is
// variadic, so an accepted value beside a refused one must still read as one
// mistake at one position.
Core\Debug::dump($plain, $s, $t);

// The other base, and the two bits composed — `secret` is one axis and
// `tainted` never launders it (`rule:security/secret-qualifier`).
Core\Debug::dump($sb);
Core\Debug::dump($st);

// `rule:errors/record-transformations`: the renderings are non-bypassable — there is no `dumpRaw` and
// no rendering selected by an argument — so the member that answers the same
// record's text as a carrier is refused on the same terms. Otherwise
// `echo Core\Debug::render($secret)` is the way round both this row and § 4's
// terminal bullet.
echo Core\Debug::render($s), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0724]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot be passed to `Core\Debug::dump`; a dump is written to be read by a person, so the value would be disclosed rather than used
   --> case.nvs:23:18
